med for the JT 2+ states. Those energy curves have two local minima at 8 ~ 0.2 and 0.5, which are labeled GS and SD minima; respectively; because the GCM results show that these minima correspond to the GS and the SD states; respectively; as explained later. In the energy curves projected to positive parity states; the energy gap between the GS and SD minima is approximately 6.7 MeV, while the gap decreases to 2.5 MeV after the AMP to JT = 0+ states.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":209.66666666666663,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":69.33333333333333,"r":540.3333333333334,"t":328.3333333333333},"charspan":[0,460],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/27","text":"performed for the JT 2+ states. Those energy curves have two local minima at 8 ~ 0.2 and 0.5, which are labeled GS and SD minima; respectively; because the GCM results show that these minima correspond to the GS and the SD states; respectively; as explained later. In the energy curves projected to positive parity states; the energy gap between the GS and SD minima is approximately 6.7 MeV, while the gap decreases to 2.5 MeV after the AMP to JT = 0+ states."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"The values of triaxiality for protons and those for neutrons are similar in the entire region (the middle panel of 1) . In the small B region; the system forms an oblate with ~ while in the large B region around the SD minimum; it forms a triaxial deformation with Density distributions of the obtained wave function at the SD are shown in Fig. 2. Because of triaxial deformations around the SD minimum;  each wave function contains different K   components; and two 2+ states are obtained after diagonalizing those different I components; i.e., K-mixing Fig; shape","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":60.66666666666663,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":71.0,"r":541.0,"t":203.33333333333337},"charspan":[0,565],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/28","text":"The values of triaxiality for protons and those for neutrons are similar in the entire region (the middle panel of 1) . In the small B region; the system forms an oblate with ~ while in the large B region around the SD minimum; it forms a triaxial deformation with Density distributions of the obtained wave function at the SD are shown in Fig. 2. Because of triaxial deformations around the SD minimum;  each wave function contains different K   components; and two 2+ states are obtained after diagonalizing those different I components; i.e., K-mixing Fig; shape"}],"version":"1.10.0"}